The exact spinon dispersions contain an energy scale \(N^{-2}\), despite the bond norm being one. My best thermodynamic estimate is
\[
T^*_{0.1}\sim \frac{6.11}{N^2},\qquad
\ell^*\sim1.03N,\qquad
\sigma^*\sim\frac{0.916}{\log N}.
\]
These large-\(N\) coefficients are conditional on a fermionic scaling limit motivated below. I have not established that limit by a controlled expansion of the full thermodynamic Bethe ansatz. The finite-\(N\) estimates below come from numerical TBA and have a firmer basis.

I used no external sources. The recalled inputs are the nested Bethe equations and the thermodynamic string construction. I derive the ground-state formulas and dispersions from them.

1. Ground state and velocity

Write
\[
e_n(\lambda)=\frac{\lambda+in/2}{\lambda-in/2},\qquad
a_n(\lambda)=\frac{n}{2\pi(\lambda^2+n^2/4)}.
\]
The nested Bethe equations in the normalization of the question are
\[
e_1(\lambda_j^{(r)})^{L\delta_{r1}}
=
\prod_{k\ne j}^{M_r}e_2(\lambda_j^{(r)}-\lambda_k^{(r)})
\prod_{k=1}^{M_{r-1}}e_{-1}(\lambda_j^{(r)}-\lambda_k^{(r-1)})
\prod_{k=1}^{M_{r+1}}e_{-1}(\lambda_j^{(r)}-\lambda_k^{(r+1)}),
\]
with \(M_0=M_N=0\), and
\[
E=L-\sum_{j=1}^{M_1}
\frac{1}{(\lambda_j^{(1)})^2+1/4}.
\]
I am taking these equations as input, rather than deriving the nested algebraic Bethe ansatz from the permutation \(R\)-matrix.

In the singlet thermodynamic ground state, the real-root densities obey
\[
\rho_r+a_2*\rho_r-a_1*(\rho_{r-1}+\rho_{r+1})
=a_1\delta_{r1},
\qquad \rho_0=\rho_N=0.
\]
With \(\widehat a_n(\omega)=e^{-n|\omega|/2}\), division by \(\widehat a_1\) gives
\[
2\cosh\frac{\omega}{2}\,\widehat\rho_r
-\widehat\rho_{r-1}-\widehat\rho_{r+1}
=\delta_{r1}.
\]
Solving this finite difference equation,
\[
\widehat\rho_r(\omega)
=\frac{\sinh((N-r)|\omega|/2)}
{\sinh(N|\omega|/2)}.
\]
Fourier inversion yields
\[
\boxed{\rho_r(\lambda)=
\frac{\sin(\pi r/N)}
{N[\cosh(2\pi\lambda/N)-\cos(\pi r/N)]}.}
\]
In particular,
\[
\int\rho_r\,d\lambda=\frac{N-r}{N},
\]
as required by the ground-state Bethe root numbers.

The ground-state energy is
\[
e_0=1-2\pi\int a_1(\lambda)\rho_1(\lambda)\,d\lambda.
\]
Substituting the Fourier transforms,
\[
e_0
=1-2\int_0^\infty
\frac{e^{-\omega}-e^{-N\omega}}{1-e^{-N\omega}}\,d\omega.
\]
Expanding the denominator and integrating term by term,
\[
\begin{aligned}
e_0
&=1-2\sum_{k=0}^\infty
\left[\frac1{Nk+1}-\frac1{N(k+1)}\right]\\
&=\boxed{1+\frac2N[\psi(1/N)+\gamma]}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The expansion
\[
\psi(z)=-\gamma-\frac1z+
\sum_{m=1}^\infty(-1)^{m+1}\zeta(m+1)z^m
\]
therefore gives
\[
\boxed{
e_0=-1+\frac{\pi^2}{3N^2}
-\frac{2\zeta(3)}{N^3}
+\frac{\pi^4}{45N^4}
+O(N^{-5}).
}
\]

To obtain the velocity, remove one root from sea \(r\). Dressing its energy and momentum with the same linear integral operator gives
\[
\epsilon_r(\lambda)=2\pi\rho_r(\lambda),
\qquad
\left|\frac{dp_r}{d\lambda}\right|=2\pi\rho_r(\lambda).
\]
At either rapidity endpoint,
\[
\rho_r(\lambda)\propto e^{-2\pi|\lambda|/N}.
\]
Thus
\[
v=\lim_{|\lambda|\to\infty}
\left|\frac{d\epsilon_r/d\lambda}{dp_r/d\lambda}\right|
=\boxed{\frac{2\pi}{N}}.
\]
This is independent of \(r\) and exact, so there are no subleading terms in its large-\(N\) expansion.

For \(N=2\),
\[
v=\pi,\qquad
e_0=1+\psi(1/2)+\gamma=1-2\log2,
\]
which checks the Hamiltonian normalization. Also, \(e_0\to-1\) from above, consistent with the lower bound on each bond.

2. Spinons and the failure of linear dispersion

There are \(N-1\) elementary hole branches. A rank-\(r\) hole carries the antisymmetric representation \(\wedge^r\mathbf N\), of dimension \(\binom Nr\). Physical states on a periodic ring obey additional representation and Bethe-number constraints. These branches should not be counted as independent gases with unrestricted occupations.

Define
\[
\alpha_r=\pi\frac{N-r}{N}.
\]
Integrating the momentum derivative gives a momentum interval
\[
0\le p\le2\alpha_r.
\]
For example, choosing momentum decreasing with rapidity,
\[
p(\lambda)=\alpha_r
-2\arctan\!\left[
\cot\frac{\pi r}{2N}\,
\tanh\frac{\pi\lambda}{N}
\right].
\]
Eliminating \(\lambda\),
\[
\boxed{
\epsilon_r(p)=\frac{2\pi}{N}
\frac{\cos(p-\alpha_r)-\cos\alpha_r}{\sin\alpha_r},
\qquad 0\le p\le2\alpha_r.
}
\]
Both endpoint slopes have magnitude \(2\pi/N\).

The maximum is at \(p=\alpha_r\), so the bandwidth is
\[
\boxed{
W_r=\frac{2\pi}{N}\cot\frac{\pi r}{2N}.
}
\]
Its different large-\(N\) regimes are
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{branch}&\text{momentum width}&W_r\\ \hline
r\text{ fixed}&2\pi-O(N^{-1})&
\displaystyle\frac4r-\frac{\pi^2r}{3N^2}+O(N^{-4})\\[2mm]
r/N\to x\in(0,1)&2\pi(1-x)&
\displaystyle\frac{2\pi}{N}\cot\frac{\pi x}{2}\\[2mm]
r=N-k,\ k\text{ fixed}&2\pi k/N&
\displaystyle\frac{\pi^2k}{N^2}+O(N^{-4})
\end{array}
\]

Bandwidth and linearity scale are different. Expanding about an endpoint,
\[
\epsilon_r(q)
=vq-\frac v2\cot\alpha_r\,q^2-\frac v6q^3+\cdots.
\]
Consequently,

- For \(r/N\) bounded away from the endpoints, linearity fails at \(q=O(1)\), hence energy \(O(N^{-1})\).
- For \(r\) or \(N-r\) fixed, it fails already at \(q=O(N^{-1})\), hence energy \(O(N^{-2})\).

The two fundamental/conjugate branches make the latter scale particularly transparent:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\epsilon_1(q)&=\frac{2\pi}{N}q+q^2+\cdots,\\
\epsilon_{N-1}(q)&=\frac{2\pi}{N}q-q^2+\cdots,
\qquad 0\le q\le\frac{2\pi}{N}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The first has an \(O(1)\) bandwidth but an \(O(N^{-2})\) endpoint linearity scale.

For \(N=2\), the general formula reduces to
\[
\epsilon(p)=\pi\sin p,\qquad 0\le p\le\pi,
\]
another normalization check.

3. Thermodynamics, numerical results, and the large-\(N\) gap

First, a specification of the TBA used for the numerical estimates.

Let \(Y_{r,n}=\rho^h_{r,n}/\rho_{r,n}\), where \(r=1,\ldots,N-1\) and \(n\ge1\) is the string length. Define
\[
K(\lambda)=\frac1{2\cosh\pi\lambda}.
\]
The partially decoupled equations are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log Y_{r,n}
={}&-\frac{2\pi}{T}K\,\delta_{r1}\delta_{n1}\\
&+K*\left[
\log(1+Y_{r,n-1})+\log(1+Y_{r,n+1})\right.\\
&\hspace{24mm}\left.
-\log(1+Y_{r-1,n}^{-1})
-\log(1+Y_{r+1,n}^{-1})
\right].
\end{aligned}
\]
The boundary conventions are \(Y_{r,0}=0\) and \(Y_{0,n}^{-1}=Y_{N,n}^{-1}=0\).

Here is how these equations follow from the string construction. The string-density constraints have the form
\[
a_n\delta_{r1}
=\rho^h_{r,n}+\sum_m A_{nm}*\rho_{r,m}
-\sum_m B_{nm}*(\rho_{r-1,m}+\rho_{r+1,m}),
\]
where
\[
\begin{aligned}
A_{nm}&=\delta_{nm}\delta+(1-\delta_{nm})a_{|n-m|}
+2\sum_{j=1}^{\min(n,m)-1}a_{|n-m|+2j}+a_{n+m},\\
B_{nm}&=\sum_{j=1}^{\min(n,m)}a_{|n-m|+2j-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Varying energy minus \(T\) times the Yang–Yang entropy,
\[
s_{\rm YY}=\sum_{r,n}\int
\left[(\rho+\rho^h)\log(\rho+\rho^h)
-\rho\log\rho-\rho^h\log\rho^h\right],
\]
gives the coupled TBA. Applying
\[
C_{nm}=\delta_{nm}\delta-K(\delta_{n,m-1}+\delta_{n,m+1})
\]
uses the identities \(CA=I\), \(CB=K I\), and \(Ca=K\delta_{n1}\), producing the displayed decoupled equations.

The free energy can be written
\[
\boxed{
f(T)=e_0-T\sum_{r=1}^{N-1}
\int\rho_r(\lambda)\log(1+Y_{r,1}(\lambda))\,d\lambda.
}
\]
Thus this calculation includes the marginal corrections automatically.

For the entropy, I differentiated these equations rather than differencing free energies. If
\[
x_{r,n}=\log Y_{r,n},\qquad
z_{r,n}=T\partial_Tx_{r,n},
\]
then
\[
s=\sum_r\int\rho_r
\left[
\log(1+e^{x_{r,1}})
+\frac{e^{x_{r,1}}}{1+e^{x_{r,1}}}z_{r,1}
\right].
\]
Differentiating the TBA supplies a linear integral equation for \(z\).

As a check, the infinite-temperature constant solution is
\[
Y_{r,n}=\frac{n(n+N)}{r(N-r)}.
\]
Substitution into the free-energy expression gives \(f/T\to-\log N\). Independently, the infinite-temperature variance per bond is \(1-N^{-2}\), since covariances between distinct bonds vanish. Hence
\[
s(T)=\log N-\frac{1-N^{-2}}{2T^2}+O(T^{-3}).
\]
For \(N=2,T=100\), the numerical result was
\[
s=0.693109434456,
\]
compared with \(0.693109680560\) from the displayed expansion.

The main onset calculations used 40 strings, 48 for \(N=2\), and 1024 rapidity points. Integral-equation residuals were below \(2\times10^{-9}\). These residuals are not estimates of the string-truncation error. Separate cutoff checks support roughly three significant digits in the table below.

The controlled bound supplied by entropy saturation becomes
\[
\boxed{
T^*_{0.1}\le
\frac{6\log N}{0.9N(N-1)},\qquad
\ell^*\ge\frac{0.9\pi(N-1)}{3\log N}.
}
\]
The exact dispersions alone do not improve this to a thermodynamic \(N^{-2}\) bound.

The following gives the stronger, but conditional, estimate.

Set \(k_F=\pi/N\). The fundamental particle and hole dispersions found above approach
\[
\epsilon_{\rm particle}(q)=(k_F+q)^2-k_F^2,
\qquad
\epsilon_{\rm hole}(q)=k_F^2-(k_F-q)^2.
\]
This suggests \(N\) fermion flavors with dispersion \(k^2\), density \(1/N\) per flavor, and fixed total density one.

This identification is the heuristic step. Matching one-particle dispersions does not determine thermodynamics. In particular, the growing color and string sums must be controlled before weak individual scattering can justify an ideal-gas limit.

Conditional on that reduction, the rest follows without further assumptions. Write
\[
t=N^2T,\qquad k=q/N,\qquad \mu=m/N^2.
\]
The density constraint becomes
\[
1=\frac1\pi\int_0^\infty
\frac{dq}{1+\exp[(q^2-m)/t]}.
\]
Define \(n_q=[1+\exp((q^2-m)/t)]^{-1}\). Then
\[
f(T)=-1+\frac{\mathcal F(t)}{N^2}+o(N^{-2}),
\]
where
\[
\mathcal F(t)=m-\frac t\pi\int_0^\infty
\log(1+e^{(m-q^2)/t})\,dq,
\]
and
\[
s(T)=\mathcal S(t)+o(1),\qquad
\mathcal S(t)=\frac1\pi\int_0^\infty
[-n_q\log n_q-(1-n_q)\log(1-n_q)]\,dq.
\]
The derivative of \(m(t)\) cancels from \(-\mathcal F'(t)\) by the density constraint.

There are two useful analytic checks. At zero temperature, \(m=\pi^2\), giving
\[
\mathcal F(0)=\frac1\pi\int_0^\pi q^2\,dq=\frac{\pi^2}{3}.
\]
This reproduces the leading exact ground-state energy. A Sommerfeld expansion gives
\[
1=\frac{\sqrt m}{\pi}
-\frac{\pi t^2}{24m^{3/2}}+\cdots,
\qquad
m=\pi^2+\frac{t^2}{12}+\cdots,
\]
and consequently
\[
\boxed{
\mathcal S(t)=\frac t6+\frac{t^3}{45\pi^2}+\cdots.
}
\]
The leading term agrees with
\[
s_{\rm CFT}=\frac{N(N-1)T}{6}
=\frac t6(1-N^{-1}).
\]

Numerical quadrature gives
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
t&\mathcal S(t)&6\mathcal S(t)/t\\ \hline
2&0.3542649212&1.0627947635\\
6&0.9059908102&0.9059908102
\end{array}
\]
and
\[
\max_t\frac{6\mathcal S(t)}t
=1.06946707,\qquad t_{\rm max}=2.45739154.
\]
Thus the limiting candidate never exceeds the upper 10% tolerance. Its first failure is the lower crossing:
\[
\frac{6\mathcal S(a)}a=0.9,\qquad
a=6.1079521206.
\]
It predicts
\[
\boxed{
T^*_{0.1}\sim\frac{6.10795}{N^2},\quad
\ell^*\sim1.02869N,\quad
\sigma^*\sim\frac{0.916193}{\log N}.
}
\]

The finite-\(N\) TBA supports this candidate. At \(t=2\), the ratios are
\[
1.08139286\quad(N=8),\qquad
1.07395687\quad(N=16),
\]
approaching the candidate value \(1.06279476\). At \(t=6\), the \(N=16\) result is \(0.91374248\), compared with \(0.90599081\).

This evidence favors \(N^{-2}\) over \((\log N)/N^2\). It is not a controlled proof excluding the latter. Establishing the uniform large-\(N\) reduction of the TBA remains the central unfinished step.

In the intermediate regime, the same candidate has a classical dilute-gas limit. For \(t\gg1\), its fugacity satisfies
\[
1\simeq \frac z\pi\int_0^\infty e^{-q^2/t}\,dq
=\frac{z\sqrt t}{2\sqrt\pi},
\]
so \(z\simeq2\sqrt{\pi/t}\). Therefore
\[
\boxed{
s(T)\simeq
\log(N\sqrt T)+\frac32-\log(2\sqrt\pi)
=\log(N\sqrt T)+0.23448788
}
\]
in the joint regime \(N^{-2}\ll T\ll1\), conditional on the same reduction. In particular, if \(T=N^{-\alpha}\), \(0<\alpha<2\),
\[
\frac{s(T)}{\log N}\longrightarrow1-\frac\alpha2.
\]
The entropy would therefore leave its linear CFT regime while still occupying a vanishing fraction of the on-site entropy.

The short-segment observation is consistent with this picture but does not prove it. Stirling’s formula gives, for \(1\ll L\ll N\),
\[
\frac1L\log\binom NL
=\log\frac NL+1-\frac{\log(2\pi L)}{2L}
-\frac{L-1}{2N}+\cdots.
\]
For \(L=xN\), \(0<x<1\), it instead tends to \(h(x)/x\), which is \(O(1)\). Choosing a quadratic-dispersion thermal length \(L\sim T^{-1/2}\) reproduces the \(\log(N\sqrt T)\) dependence.

There is an energetic obstruction to turning this into a block argument. Joining isolated blocks generally costs \(O(1/L)\) per site. At \(L\sim N\), that exceeds the proposed \(N^{-2}\) thermal scale. The segment entropy alone is insufficient.

4. Finite volume

For the identity-sector comparison, take \(L\) divisible by \(N\). Other congruence classes require the appropriate nontrivial charge sector, rather than the identity vacuum.

The CFT prediction is
\[
E-E_{\rm bulk}
=\frac{2\pi v}{L}\left(\Delta-\frac c{12}\right),
\]
with marginal logarithmic corrections.

A momentum displacement carrying \(m\) conformal units has
\[
q\sim\frac{2\pi m}{L}.
\]
For the branches with the smallest linearity scale,
\[
\frac{|\epsilon-vq|}{vq}
\sim\frac{q}{v}
\sim\frac{mN}{L}.
\]
Thus fixed conformal levels suggest
\[
L\gg N.
\]

Uniform control through
\[
\Delta_{\max}\simeq \frac c4+2=O(N)
\]
is stronger. The spectrum includes descendants putting \(m=O(N)\) units into a single momentum. For those states,
\[
\frac{|\epsilon-vq|}{vq}=O(N^2/L).
\]
My resulting heuristic criterion is
\[
\boxed{L\gg N^2}
\]
for every level in that window to have small relative dispersion corrections.

This is a worst-state criterion. Primary families and states distributing their conformal energy among many modes can approach CFT behavior on shorter rings. Marginal logarithms provide a separate limitation, so neither \(L\gg N\) nor \(L\gg N^2\) specifies a universal numerical accuracy threshold.

5. Numerical table

The finite-\(N\) onset estimates include marginal corrections through the TBA.

| \(N\) | \(v\) | \(e_0\) | \(T^*_{0.1}\) | \(\ell^*\) | \(\sigma^*\) |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 2 | 3.141593 | −0.386294 | 0.475 | 6.61 | 0.251 |
| 3 | 2.094395 | −0.703212 | 0.213 | 9.82 | 0.214 |
| 4 | 1.570796 | −0.825119 | 0.128 | 12.3 | 0.203 |
| 5 | 1.256637 | −0.884730 | 0.0881 | 14.3 | 0.201 |
| 8 | 0.785398 | −0.952819 | 0.0995 | 7.89 | 0.402 |
| Large \(N\), conditional thermodynamics | \(2\pi/N\) | \(-1+\pi^2/(3N^2)-2\zeta(3)/N^3+\cdots\) | \(6.108/N^2\) | \(1.029N\) | \(0.9162/\log N\) |

The nonmonotonicity between \(N=5\) and \(N=8\) follows from the definition. For \(N=2,3,4,5\), the first failure is an upward crossing of \(1.1\). For \(N=8\), the overshoot stays below 10%, and the first failure is the later downward crossing of \(0.9\).

For numerical checks, increasing the \(N=2\) cutoff to 80 strings and 2048 rapidity points gave \(s/s_{\rm CFT}=1.09999865\) at the quoted root. For \(N=8\), increasing from 32 to 64 strings changed the ratio at \(t=2.5\) from \(1.09259108\) to \(1.09245282\). At \(T=0.0995\), the 64-string result was \(0.89993091\). These checks support the quoted precision and the distinction between the two types of crossing.

The scripts and results are saved in the working directory as `tba_check.py`, `roots_tba.py`, `scan_tba.py`, `ideal_gas.py`, and `STATE.md`. I stopped an additional \(N=32\) cutoff check to respect the runtime limit; it is not used as a converged benchmark.

The exact BA results and finite-\(N\) onset estimates are established to the qualifications above. The remaining research task is to derive, or replace, the proposed fermionic large-\(N\) scaling function.

